\par\par \b \uc2 Chile Peso Ends Stronger On Rising Copper Prices, Dollar Sales\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Esposito   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 373 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Mai 2011\par 20:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--The Chilean peso closed stronger versus the dollar Monday on the back of rising international copper prices and institutional dollar sales.\par \par \uc2 The peso gained to CLP467.20 to the dollar, versus Friday's close of CLP467.80, while trading in a range of CLP466.80 to CLP469.00.\par \par \uc2 Spot copper prices in London trading rose 1.3% to $4.04060 per pound, according to Chile's state copper commission Cochilco, on bargain-hunting by investors.\par \par \uc2 Because Chile is the world's largest copper producer, accounting for a third of global output, its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  often takes direction from the industrial metal's prices abroad.\par \par \uc2 The euro's slip against the dollar, however, tempered the peso's strength. Investors sold euros after \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  downgrade of Greece's credit rating.\par \par \uc2 As Chile's economy is highly export-intensive and almost a third of its exports are bound for European ports, the peso often moves in the same direction as the euro does against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 "The market was selling dollars today...but if over the coming sessions copper's rebound sputters and the euro stays flat, the peso will be stuck in a tight range," said Osvaldo Molina, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at local brokerage and investment bank LarrainVial.\par \par \uc2 The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely see resistance at CLP462-CLP465 and support at CLP470-CLP472 over the coming days, he added.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as part of the its $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, purchased $50 million on Monday at an average rate of CLP468.29. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has accumulated $4.4 billion so far this year.\par \par \uc2 In the bond market, yields on inflation-indexed Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, or BCUs, ended lower on institutional demand for fixed income instruments.\par \par \uc2 The yield on five-year BCU bonds ended at 2.58%, from 2.62% on Friday, while the yield on 10-year BCUs closed at 2.88%, versus 2.90% the prior session.\par \par \uc2 (Peso and bond quotes provided by Valor Futuro newswire.)\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Esposito, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8929; anthony.esposito@dowjones.com [ 09-05-11 1803GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1170 1844   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110509e759001dd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
