\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-No pressure from Chile's blue-chips on peso surge\b0\par\par\uc2 897 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Mai 2011\par 19:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 * Strong peso boosts many bottom lines, easing push to act\par \par \uc2 * Exporters stay profitable with commodities boom\par \par \uc2 * After \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , options to tame peso scarce\par \par \uc2 By Brad Haynes\par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO, May 5 (Reuters) - Chilean policymakers can hold off aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures like those in Brazil as the peso's surge to three-year highs punishes smaller exporters but proves a blessing for major firms riding a consumption-driven boom.\par \par \uc2 Chile's top-40 IPSA stock index <.IPSA> is dominated by retailers, banks and electric companies that have surged with greater purchasing power and domestic demand. Top exporters, meanwhile, are cashing in on record prices for raw materials.\par \par \uc2 In the lower-profile fruit sector, where global prices have not offset the waning weight of weak-dollar revenues, some exporters are howling for government measures and elevated \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- while the economy's heavyweights stay silent.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso <CLP=CL> has rallied 17 percent since mid-2010 and 3 percent in the past six weeks, erasing the impact of a $12 billion 2011 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program launched in January due to global dollar weakness and the strength of the country's main export, copper.\par \par \uc2 While regional peer Brazil has implemented a series of unorthodox measures to protect local industry from \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength, Chilean authorities have repeatedly waved off capital controls, opening the door for an extended rally.\par \par \uc2 "Chilean authorities haven't succumbed to political pressures," said analyst Eduardo Suarez of \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2 , citing one reason he was shorting Brazil's real against Chile's peso. "In Brazil they're getting much more pressure to act." <^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ > Chile stocks unfazed by \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  rally {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://r.reuters.com/vyd49r"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://r.reuters.com/vyd49r }}}\uc2  > Options scarce to tame Chile's peso [ID:nN26276247] > For a TAKE A LOOK on Chile economy [ID:nN26HILEFI] ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^>\par \par \uc2 It helps that so many leading Chilean firms see the brighter side of cheaper imports.\par \par \uc2 "A lower exchange rate is clearly favorable for consumption," said Juan Benavides, chief executive of leading retailer Falabella , Chile's biggest company by bourse capitalization. Benavides noted that a strong peso also helped with higher global prices for inputs like cotton.\par \par \uc2 Regional airline LAN's cargo and passenger traffic has soared with greater purchasing power in Chile and Brazil, offsetting higher local costs due to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength, according to Chief Financial Officer Alejandro de la Fuente.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, state mining giant Codelco, the world's No. 1 copper producer, accepts \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation as a side effect of the boom lifting all of Chile's major commodity exporters.\par \par \uc2 "It's not a serious problem," Codelco Chairman Gerardo Jofre told Reuters at a business conference this week. "Costs indexed to the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  are rising, but of course that's counteracted by high copper prices."\par \par \uc2 Strong wood pulp prices also boosted the bottom line for industrial conglomerate Copec , the IPSA's top-weighted stock, whose profit rose 76 percent last year from 2009, while forestry rival CMPC saw a 144 percent jump.\par \par \uc2 Raw materials make up about two-thirds of Chilean exports according to UN data, but manufactured goods make up just one-tenth -- compared with more than a third of Brazil's exports and 75 percent of Mexico's.\par \par \uc2 In Mexico, industries competing with a flood of cheap Chinese goods have begun protesting the local peso's rise even though it remains undervalued by several metrics, according to RBC's Suarez.\par \par \uc2 Brazilian policymakers have cited the vulnerability of local manufacturers as they beat the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  back from record highs with taxes and reserve requirements on incoming capital. [ID:nN29268075]\par \par \uc2 Chile's chamber of commerce has remained silent on the peso's appreciation to three-year highs last week.\par \par \uc2 The Chilean companies hit hardest by the peso's rise, such as fruit and berry farmers, are smaller and less visible in Santiago, the capital. They have seen costs rising in dollar terms while global prices for their goods are flat.\par \par \uc2 "Our sector doesn't get much attention. There's a political indifference," said Antonio Walker, who runs fruit trade group Fedefruta, as well his orchards in the sparsely populated south. "Chileans have gone from agricultural exporters to a nation of mining, retail and construction."\par \par \uc2 Fedefruta has adjusted demands accordingly. While some farmers still demand a heavier government hand in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, Walker is asking for more targeted lifelines aimed at farmers' hiring, energy and credit costs.\par \par \uc2 "Since we have a sector-specific problem, we're asking for sector-specific measures," he said.\par \par \uc2 The government has responded with programs to help small exporters with \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hedging and cut red tape on customs. But administration and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials continue to express skepticism about capital controls. [ID:nN08100714]\par \par \uc2 Analysts see the government maintaining more farmer-friendly measures and encouraging capital outflows before turning to more unorthodox \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures in the case of a severely misaligned peso. For options to tame the peso: [ID:nN26276247]\par \par \uc2 But Chile's breaking point may be farther off than regional peers'.\par \par \uc2 The peso closed at 468.50 per U.S. dollar on Thursday. But in 2008, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  waited until the exchange rate firmed past 430 per dollar before \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Alonso Soto in Santiago and Luciana Lopez in Sao Paulo and Jeb Blount in Rio de Janeiro)\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Simon Gardner and Dan Grebler)\par \par \uc2 CHILE-PESO/ (ANALYSIS, GRAPHIC)  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110508e755000il\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
