\par\par \b \uc2 Chile Stocks Close End 1.1% Lower Following US Market Slump\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Esposito   \par \uc2 of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 522 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Mai 2011\par 21:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--Chile's blue-chip Ipsa index ended 1.1% lower Wednesday, pulled down by sliding U.S. markets and the International Monetary Fund's warning about future stock-market bubbles and overheating risks in countries such as Argentina, Chile and Brazil.\par \par \uc2 The Ipsa fell to 4755 points, while market volume fell to 89.5 billion Chilean pesos ($192.3 million), down from CLP102.8 billion the previous session.\par \par \uc2 As several of the Ipsa's heavier-weighted shares also trade in New York, the Ipsa often tracks the Dow Jones Industrial Average. On the back of disappointing U.S. economic data, the DJIA recently dropped 89 points, or 0.7%, to 12718.\par \par \uc2 Among dually listed shares, chemical and specialty fertilizer producer SQM's (SQM) more liquid B-series (SQM-B.SN) declined 2.3% to CLP26,700 and the nation's biggest bank in terms of loans \uc2 Banco Santander Chile\uc2  (SAN, BSANTANDE.SN) decreased 1.6% to CLP40.50.\par \par \uc2 Earlier in the session, Miguel Savastano, the deputy director of the Western Hemisphere Department of the IMF, reiterated that Chile and other South American nations were facing overheating pressures. Savastano added that while the Ipsa was currently overvalued, if the local market continued rising, in "about six months or so, it's going to start showing symptoms again of being overvalued."\par \par \uc2 The IMF's warning had a "big impact on local sentiment and combined with slumping international equity markets, helped to pull the Ipsa lower," said Cristina Acle, head of studies with local brokerage Corpbanca.\par \par \uc2 Also, commodities-related companies fell sharply as copper prices dropped during the session.\par \par \uc2 Integrated steel and iron ore producer Cap (CAP.SN) lost 2% to CLP23,901 and fuel and forestry conglomerate Copec (COPEC.SN) shed 1.5% to CLP8,765.\par \par \uc2 In other market news, the peso ended 1% weaker versus the dollar, as disappointing economic data in the U.S. and U.K. pressured international copper prices and equity markets lower.\par \par \uc2 The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened to CLP465.50 to the dollar, moving away from a recent three-year higher, versus Tuesday's close of CLP461.00. The peso traded in a broad CLP460.95 to CLP466.10 range.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as part of the its $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, purchased $50 million on Wednesday at an average rate of CLP461.18. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has accumulated $4.25 billion so far this year.\par \par \uc2 In the bond market, yields on inflation-indexed Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, or BCUs, ended higher as expectations for medium to long term inflation have moderated on slipping international crude oil prices, said a local fixed-income trader.\par \par \uc2 The yield on five-year BCU bonds ended at 2.60% versus 2.59% on Tuesday, while the yield on 10-year BCUs closed at 2.89%, from 2.87% the prior session.\par \par \uc2 (Peso and bond quotes provided by Valor Futuro newswire.)\par \par \uc2 --By Anthony Esposito, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8929; anthony.esposito@dowjones.com [ 04-05-11 1923GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5057   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110504e7540019j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
