\par\par \b \uc2 Chile Stocks End 1.3% Higher On Latest Batch Of Economic Data\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Esposito   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 495 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 April 2011\par 21:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--Chile's blue-chip Ipsa index ended 1.3% higher Friday, following the latest batch of strong economic data that pointed to the nation's continued quick-paced growth.\par \par \uc2 The Ipsa jumped to 4818.97 points, while market volume slipped to 73.3 billion Chilean pesos ($159.4 million) from CLP84.7 billion the previous session.\par \par \uc2 Earlier in the session, the government statistics agency reported that, for a third consecutive three-month period, unemployment in Chile held steady at 7.3%. The prior session, the agency reported that industrial output surged a record 30.9% in March from the same month a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 The government has said that Chile's monthly gross domestic proxy index, known as the Imacec, will post double-digit growth in March in light of the robust data.\par \par \uc2 Analysts are expecting the Imacec to surge between 11% and 21% in March.\par \par \uc2 "Firm economic data is boosting share prices," said Gutenberg Martinez, equity analyst with local brokerage Corpbanca Corredores de Bolsa.\par \par \uc2 Among gainers, retailers, which are benefiting from robust domestic demand, rose sharply. Within the sector, La Polar (LAPOLAR.SN) rose 0.9% to CLP2,585.10, after its first quarter earnings jumped 59% to CLP6.21 billion and beat expectations.\par \par \uc2 Retailers Hites (HITES.SN) jumped 2.5% to CLP738.00, and Ripley (RIPLEY.SN) gained 1.5% to CLP618.00.\par \par \uc2 Also rising, flagship carrier \uc2 LAN Airlines\uc2  (LFL, LAN.SN) advanced 1.3% to CLP12,951.00, and fuel and forestry conglomerate Copec (COPEC.SN) gained 1.8% to CLP8,930.80.\par \par \uc2 In other market news, the peso ended marginally stronger versus the dollar, hitting a fresh three-year high as market participants don't expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to implement additional \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures.\par \par \uc2 The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened to CLP460.20 to the dollar from Thursday's close of CLP460.40. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded in a very narrow range of CLP459.00 to CLP460.75.\par \par \uc2 As part of its $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday purchased $50 million at an average rate of CLP460.50. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has accumulated $4.1 billion so far this year.\par \par \uc2 In the bond market, yields on inflation-indexed Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, or BCUs, ended higher as the market awaits the April consumer price index data to be released late next week.\par \par \uc2 Based on bond prices, the market sees the CPI gaining 0.4% on the month in April.\par \par \uc2 The yield on five-year BCU bonds ended at 2.56%, up from 2.55% on Thursday, while the yield on 10-year BCUs closed at 2.86%, up from 2.84%.\par \par \uc2 (Peso and bond quotes provided by Valor Futuro newswire.)\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Esposito, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8929; anthony.esposito@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 --Carolina Pica contributed to this article. [ 29-04-11 1916GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5057   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110429e74t0015d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
