\par\par \b \uc2 Chile Stocks Close Flat, Waiting For Next Round Of Earnings\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Esposito   \par \uc2 of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 495 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 April 2011\par 21:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--Chile's blue-chip Ipsa index ended virtually flat Thursday as investors awaited the next round of corporate earnings after the first batch came in mixed.\par \par \uc2 The Ipsa gained a meager 0.04% to 4756.89 points, while market volume slipped to 84.7 billion Chilean pesos ($183.9 million) from CLP88.0 billion the previous session.\par \par \uc2 Among gainers, telecommunications operator Entel (ENTEL.SN) jumped 4.2% to CLP9,100.00 as investors digested the company's plans to invest nearly $2 billion through 2013, with a focus in the mobile broadband sector.\par \par \uc2 "It's a big investment and participants are reacting to the announcement," said Gutenberg Martinez, equity analyst with local brokerage Corpbanca Corredores de Bolsa.\par \par \uc2 Also gaining, the nation's largest power producer, \uc2 Endesa\uc2  (EOC, ENDESA.SN), grew 0.2% to CLP863.90 and its parent company, power holding firm \uc2 Enersis\uc2  (ENI, ENERSIS.SN), increased 0.8% to CLP196.50, recovering from the prior session's fall.\par \par \uc2 Among decliners, flagship carrier \uc2 LAN Airlines\uc2  (LFL, LAN.SN) lost 0.2% to CLP12,820.00 after \uc2 UBS\uc2  downgraded its shares to neutral from buy, citing a "limited upside potential of 13.3% from current levels."\par \par \uc2 Also, energy holding company CGE (CGE.SN) lost 0.7% to CLP2,775.00, after the nation's energy and fuels regulator said CGE risks being fined up to $9.8 million for mistakenly overcharging its customers.\par \par \uc2 Participants are likely waiting for the next batch of earnings--"in particular, retailers"--which should benefit again from strong domestic demand, said Martinez.\par \par \uc2 In other local markets, the peso Thursday ended at a new three-year high versus the dollar, as the greenback continued its slide against major rivals after Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke on Wednesday indicated the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is far from tightening monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened to CLP460.70 to the dollar, versus Wednesday's close of CLP462.10. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded in a range of CLP459.00 to CLP461.00.\par \par \uc2 As part of its $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchased $50 million at an average rate of CLP460.18. It has accumulated $4.05 billion so far this year.\par \par \uc2 In the bond market, yields on inflation-indexed Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, or BCUs, ended mixed after the minutes of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's April monetary policy meeting said inflation in the country, including core inflation which factors out volatile fuel and food prices, will likely slow down.\par \par \uc2 The yield on five-year BCU bonds ended at 2.55%, from 2.53% on Wednesday, while the yield on 10-year BCUs closed at 2.84%, from 2.85% in the prior session.\par \par \uc2 (Peso and bond quotes provided by Valor Futuro newswire.)\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Esposito, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8929; anthony.esposito@dowjones.com [ 28-04-11 1930GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5057   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110428e74s0020l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
