\par\par \b \uc2 Chile Stocks Close Mildly Higher As Investors Await Earnings\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Esposito  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 435 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 April 2011\par 21:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--Chile's blue-chip Ipsa index ended mildly higher Monday as participants were optimistic about the start of the local earnings season.\par \par \uc2 The Ipsa gained 0.3% to 4751.74 points, while market volume grew to 57.78 billion Chilean pesos ($123.6 million), from CLP48.0 billion the previous session. All markets and government offices were closed Friday in observance of a religious holiday.\par \par \uc2 Among companies scheduled to report their earnings this week, flagship carrier \uc2 LAN Airlines\uc2  (LFL, LAN.SN) climbed 1.3% to CLP12,900.00. Analysts expected LAN's first-quarter profit to grow 36% on the year to $120 million.\par \par \uc2 The nation's largest power producer \uc2 Endesa\uc2  (EOC, ENDESA.SN) expanded 1.1% to CLP872.50 and its parent company, power holding firm \uc2 Enersis\uc2  (ENI, ENERSIS.SN), increased a similar 1.1% to CLP198.00.\par \par \uc2 Industry analysts expect for a long-standing drought to draw to a close soon, and cheaper hydroelectric power generation to increase, as the Nina weather phenomenon fizzles out.\par \par \uc2 Also gaining, rival power producer \uc2 Colbun (COLBUN.SN) \uc2 rose 1.1% to CLP134.00.\par \par \uc2 "The end of the Nina [phenomenon] will bode well for power generators and for their bottom lines, which have suffered in recent quarters," said Rodrigo Mujica, analyst with local brokerage BCI Corredor de Bolsa.\par \par \uc2 In other local markets, the peso ended weaker against the dollar, slipping from a strong level of resistance, after almost breaching pre-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels the prior session.\par \par \uc2 The peso slipped to CLP467.40 to the dollar, versus Thursday's close of CLP466.50, while trading in tight a range of CLP466.80 to CLP468.30.\par \par \uc2 As part of its $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchased $50 million at an average rate of CLP467.66 Monday. It has accumulated $3.90 billion so far this year.\par \par \uc2 In the bond market, yields on inflation-indexed Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, or BCUs, ended higher in thin trading as fixed-income participants awaited the U.S. Federal Reserve's policy meeting and Chairman Ben Bernanke's first-ever post policy meeting press conference later in the week.\par \par \uc2 The yield on five-year BCU bonds ended at 2.46%, compared to 2.44% on Thursday, while the yield on 10-year BCUs closed at 2.84%, versus 2.83% the prior session.\par \par \uc2 (Peso and bond quotes provided by Valor Futuro newswire.)\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Esposito, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8929; anthony.esposito@dowjones.com [ 25-04-11 1910GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5057  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110425e74p000ng\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
