\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS- \highlight22\uc2 Chile \highlight\uc2  peso climbs to pre- \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  high\b0\par\par\uc2 553 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 April 2011\par 19:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Strong earnings, risk appetite boosts Latam \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  outlook\par \par \uc2 * Slower Brazil rate hikes not seen stopping real rally\par \par \uc2 * Mexican peso choppy around 30-month high\par \par \uc2 * Easter holidays sap trade volumes\par \par \uc2 By Brad Haynes\par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO, April 21 (Reuters) - Chile's peso firmed in thin pre-holiday trade and reached its strongest since the January launch of a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as robust U.S. and European earnings drove appetite for higher-yielding assets.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso tested new 30-month highs on Thursday as it see-sawed on low volume, settling flat ahead of the Easter holiday.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's spot \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market was closed for a four-day weekend, but one-month non-deliverable forwards <BRL1MNDFOR=> traded a shade weaker after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  slowed the pace of its rate hikes late Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised its benchmark rate by 25 basis points to 12 percent in a split decision, backing off the intensity of monetary tightening and easing upward pressure on a rallying real. [ID:nN20289067]\par \par \uc2 But with Brazilian interest rates towering over yields in developed economies, analysts see the real continuing its climb past an intraday high of 1.561 per dollar on Wednesday -- its strongest since August 2008.\par \par \uc2 "The fact that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't go with 50 basis points doesn't make a difference," said Kathryn Rooney Vera, an emerging markets strategist with Bulltick Capital Markets.\par \par \uc2 The real is within 1 percent of its strongest level since the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was freely floated in 1999, hurting exporters but helping the government's fight with annual inflation nearing its 6.5 percent ceiling.\par \par \uc2 Brazil has steadily stepped up capital controls against foreign investments, but it may now be slower to fight an exchange rate helping to dampen imported price pressures.\par \par \uc2 "I think you'll probably see more leniency toward the real's appreciation," Rooney said. "The line in the sand before was 1.65 per dollar, so now it's probably 1.50 per dollar."\par \par \uc2 Markets will be watching for mention of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  releases minutes from Wednesday's meeting early next Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Mexico reports retail sales on Tuesday. The peso <MXN=> was little changed from Wednesday at 11.6074.\par \par \uc2 In Chile, where the peso <CLP=CL> closed 0.5 percent firmer, bidding 466.8 per dollar, traders are eyeing industrial output and unemployment data due next week, expecting signs of robust growth driving interest rates to over two-year highs.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could see some consolidation before breaking through to three-year highs past 465 per dollar, however, as its relative strength index is nearing overbought levels.\par \par \uc2 The peso has almost entirely clawed back from the more than 7 percent drop that followed the launch of a $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced on January 3. [ID:nN04206531]\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's bourse wrapped up trading early on Thursday afternoon at its strongest close in two weeks, tracking gains on global bourses boosted by strong earnings.\par \par \uc2 The blue-chip IPSA stock index <.IPSA> rose 0.5 percent, led by energy utilities \uc2 Enersis\uc2  and \uc2 Endesa\uc2  and wood pulp giants Copec and CMPC .\par \par \uc2 Stock markets in Argentina, Brazil, Columbia, Mexico, Panama and Venezuela were closed for Easter holidays.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Alonso Soto; Editing by Andrew Hay)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110424e74l000ib\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
