\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Chile  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Pres: Inflation Biggest Challenge\b0\par\par\uc2 442 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 April 2011\par 18:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Updates with additional comments from \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president starting in the fourth paragraph.)\par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--Inflation is the biggest challenge the Chilean economy is currently facing, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president Jose De Gregorio said Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Inflation will likely end this year at 4.3%, above the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target of 3%, plus/minus one percentage point.\par \par \uc2 "Undoubtedly, inflation has been increasing," De Gregorio said in prepared remarks delivered to the Asimet metal industry trade group.\par \par \uc2 According to the central banker, although inflation won't hit a high anywhere near the 9.9% it saw in October 2008, it still is a risk to the economy.\par \par \uc2 "High international crude oil and food prices have increased inflationary risks in Chile as well as in the rest of the world," he said.\par \par \uc2 He added that while the inflation outlook for this year, both locally and abroad, is higher for 2011, next year this outlook should retreat somewhat.\par \par \uc2 Food prices around the world will likely grow around 4% to 5% this year, so the bulk of the inflationary pressures stem from higher crude prices, he said in his remarks.\par \par \uc2 De Gregorio reiterated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue normalizing the benchmark overnight interest rate to contain inflationary pressures and avoid the propagation of second round effects.\par \par \uc2 According to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's most recent survey of analysts expectations, the benchmark overnight rate will likely end the year at around 5.75% to 6%, De Gregorio said.\par \par \uc2 The survey also shows analysts see the rate holding at around 6%, a rate most consider neutral for the Chilean economy, for most of 2012.\par \par \uc2 Last week at its monthly monetary policy meeting, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised the benchmark rate half a percentage point, as expected, to 4.5%.\par \par \uc2 Regarding the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, De Gregorio reiterated the peso would likely be trading 3% to 4% stronger against the dollar weren't it for the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He noted, like he has in previous presentations, that \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't receiving net capital inflows and that the peso's strength against the greenback stems from robust domestic growth, strong copper prices and a weak dollar.\par \par \uc2 "The dollar is at its weakest level in 40 years," De Gregorio told the trade group which represents metallurgical exporters.\par \par \uc2 He added the peso will likely weaken somewhat as the U.S. economy rebounds.\par \par \uc2 "At some point, the dollar will strengthen; now, when will that happen? It won't be tomorrow," he said.\par \par \uc2 -By Carolina Pica, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8919; carolina.pica@dowjones.com [ 20-04-11 1610GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110420e74k001d1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
