\par\par \b \uc2 REFILE-Reuters Summit-Chile hopes spending cut slows rate rise\b0\par\par\uc2 579 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 16:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (For other news from the Reuters Latin American Investment Summit, click on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.reuters.com/summit/LatinAmericanInvestment11?pid=500"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.reuters.com/summit/LatinAmericanInvestment11?pid=500}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 * Tighter public spending to help cap rates\par \par \uc2 * Finance minister sees interest rates rising further\par \par \uc2 * No commitment to 2011 sovereign bond issue\par \par \uc2 (Refiles Tuesday's story to add Reuters Insider link of interview with Chile's finance minister)\par \par \uc2 By Simon Gardner and Antonio de la Jara\par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO, March 29 (Reuters) - Chile's move to tighten fiscal spending for 2011 should help the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  ease the pace of interest rate hikes as it fends off inflationary oil prices, Finance Minister Felipe Larrain said on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 However, Chile's benchmark rate must continue to rise given external pressures on the economy, Larrain told the Reuters Latin American Investment Summit in Santiago. Less spending should help temper inflation and therefore rates.\par \par \uc2 "We can't think that a nominal rate of 4 percent is the equilibrium rate. The rate needs to keep rising," Larrain told Reuters in an interview.\par \par \uc2 "If the (lower spending) measures work, we can contribute to how gradually the rate increases and its eventual level," he added.\par \par \uc2 Larrain said last week the government would cut 2011 spending to a 5 percent increase from last year. Chile passed a 2011 budget designed to grow 5.5 percent from last year, expanding more slowly than the country's estimated 6 percent GDP growth this year. [ID:nN23270018] <^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ > For Reuters Insider video, see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/xup78r"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/xup78r}}}\par \par \uc2 ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^>\par \par \uc2 Chile is one of a host of emerging market economies from Brazil to China that have raised rates this year to fight inflation fueled by higher global commodity and food prices.\par \par \uc2 However Chile has avoided the sort of capital controls that Brazil has opted for to counter inflows of speculative capital -- a move the International Monetary Fund last week said was no substitute for "fundamental measures" such as monetary policy. [ID:nN27133061]\par \par \uc2 Chile's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  this month raised its benchmark interest rate <CLINTR=ECI> by a bigger-than-expected 50 basis points and said more hikes will follow as it prioritizes its inflation target over a more competitive exchange rate. [ID:nN17171992]\par \par \uc2 The bank is seen raising its key rate by an aggressive 50 basis points again in April as it fights price pressures, a fortnightly \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  poll of traders showed last week.\par \par \uc2 A fresh 50 basis point hike would take the rate to 4.5 percent, adding upward pressure on the peso <CLP=CL>, which rebounded from the initial impact of a $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  launched in January.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rates peaked at 8.25 percent in late 2008, before the bank slashed them to a record low of 0.5 percent in mid-2009 and held them there for around a year. Traders see the key rate stable at 6 percent even in two years' time.\par \par \uc2 Larrain added Chile's government is committed to issuing another sovereign bond by the end of its term in 2014, but has not signed off on issuing one this year.\par \par \uc2 He said he had also received "promising" signs of interest from foreign institutions keen on issuing peso-denominated "huaso" bonds, which are aimed at countering upward pressure on the peso from capital inflows.\par \par \uc2 (For top stories from the Reuters Latin American Investment Summit, see [ID:nN27135818]\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Brad Haynes and Andrew Hay)\par \par \uc2 LATAM-SUMMIT/CHILE-FINMIN (UPDATE 1, TV) (REFILE)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110402e73u000ev\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
