\par\par \b \uc2 Chile Peso Ends Flat On Institutional Investor Dollar Buys\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Esposito   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 328 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 17:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--The Chilean peso ended flat against the dollar Thursday, erasing its earlier gains, as institutional investors bought dollars.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ended at CLP486.10 to the dollar, unchanged from Wednesday, while trading in a range of CLP483.80 to CLP487.90.\par \par \uc2 The peso had been sharply stronger against the dollar in earlier trading as fears about Japan's nuclear crisis eased, international copper prices shot higher and the euro rallied versus the dollar.\par \par \uc2 As Chile is the world's premier copper producer and exporter and because Europe is one of the Andean nation's main trade partners, the peso often takes trading cues from the industrial metal's international price movements and the euro's fluctuations against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 But "dollar purchases by local banks put downward pressure on the peso," said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader Carlos Zamora at local bank Consorcio.\par \par \uc2 Institutional investors came out buying dollars as the peso reached a strong level of resistance.\par \par \uc2 "Despite everything going in favor of the peso strengthening, rising copper prices, a stronger euro, etc., participants bought dollars after the peso neared resistance," said Sergio Tricio, head of studies at \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, as part of its $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchased $50 million at an average rate of CLP486.50. It has accumulated $2.60 billion so far this year.\par \par \uc2 In the bond market, yields on inflation-indexed Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, or BCUs, ended lower as institutional investors also snapped up bonds.\par \par \uc2 The yield on five-year BCU bonds ended at 2.39%, from 2.40% Wednesday, while the yield on 10-year BCUs closed at 2.87%, from 2.90% the previous session.\par \par \uc2 (Peso and bond quotes provided by Valor Futuro newswire.)\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Esposito, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8929; anthony.esposito@dowjones.com [ 17-03-11 1650GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1170 1844   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110317e73h001dg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
