\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 1-Inflation needs decisive attack: Chile cbank chief\b0\par\par\uc2 938 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 14:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * De Gregorio says must attack inflationary pressures\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2  peso back near pre-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels\par \par \uc2 * Mexico seen holding rate later on Friday\par \par \uc2 * Brazil raised interest rates on Wednesday\par \par \uc2 By Daniel Flynn and Brad Haynes\par \par \uc2 PARIS/SANTIAGO, March 4 (Reuters) - Chile's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  must attack inflation to mitigate risks from higher oil and food prices, bank president Jose De Gregorio said on Friday, seen as a signal the bank could opt for steeper interest rate hikes.\par \par \uc2 De Gregorio's comments in a speech in Paris contrasted with minutes from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's February rate-setting meeting released earlier on Friday, in which board members cautioned against "overreacting" to inflationary expectations, noting core inflation was limited.\par \par \uc2 The minutes appeared to rule out accelerating the pace of raising its interest rates, saying that it could send the wrong signal about inflation expectations and suggesting more steady, gradual rate hikes like February's unanimous 25 basis point increase are to come.\par \par \uc2 Several of the board's five members preferred in the minutes from the Feb. 17 rate-setting meeting to be "prudent" and not overreact to rising inflation expectations in light of muted underlying, or core, inflation.\par \par \uc2 However one unidentified board member said if inflation expectations increased persistently, steeper interest rate increases may be necessary.\par \par \uc2 Speaking at a global central bankers meeting in Paris, De Gregorio said not acting on inflationary pressures could hit credibility and the ability to rein in prices.\par \par \uc2 "The risk of propagation towards prices in the wider economy must be mitigated, in particular in economies like Chile's, which are producing close to their potential," De Gregorio said in the speech, a copy of which was released in Santiago.\par \par \uc2 "Not attacking inflationary pressures could affect credibility, which would have negative consequences for the future capacity to achieve stable inflation with low costs in terms of activity," he added.\par \par \uc2 The comments were viewed by some as suggesting steeper interest rate increases.\par \par \uc2 "It (De Gregorio's comments) suggests they're contemplating \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par a 50 basis point increase at the next meeting... This points\par clearly to the possibility of   steeper rate hikes ahead." said\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Matias Madrid, chief economist Banco Penta in Santiago.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brazil and Chile are among a slew of emerging market countries from India to Israel that tightened monetary policy this year to fight inflation fueled by higher global prices for food and commodities. <^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ > For full coverage of economy [ID:nN30113748] ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chile's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has repeatedly voiced concern about rising prices and signaled at February's monetary policy meeting more rate hikes would follow. [ID:nN23137413]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The board only considered a 25 basis point increase last month, according to the minutes. A decision to hold rates in January was the first pause since the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  started raising rates last June.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Several board members said that while inflation expectations had risen ... it did not seem prudent to overreact at this moment, especially because effective inflation, and in particular core inflation, were limited," the bank said in a statement.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Accelerating the pace of rate normalization after a pause in January could be confusing because it could be interpreted as an abrupt change in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's inflation forecasts, which is not the case," it added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chile's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  resumed its cycle of rate hikes despite the fact the local peso <CLP=CL> has bounced back to levels that prompted a $12 billion \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in January, hurting exporters and raising questions about what additional steps the bank and government could take. [ID:nN17130996]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chile's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was seen raising its benchmark interest rate by 25 basis points to 3.75 percent at its March monetary policy meeting, a fortnightly \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  poll of traders showed last month. [ID:nN23137413]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The bank is seen raising its benchmark rate to 5 percent in six months time', meaning the market is betting on steady interest rate increases.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Inflation quickened in Brazil and Chile in January on rising food and transport costs, putting more pressure on central bankers to push interest rates higher.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chile releases February inflation data on March 8.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chilean consumer prices are seen rising 0.2 percent in February after a 0.3 percent increase in January, according to a Reuters poll. Core inflation rose 0.1 percent in January.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With prices for main export copper near life highs and the dollar weak internationally, the peso is firming on factors beyond Chile's control.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brazil raised its interest rates on Wednesday for the second time in three months, opting for an increase in line with market expectations despite mounting concerns about the pace of inflation. [ID:nN01145534]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brazilian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policymakers voted unanimously to lift the benchmark Selic rate to 11.75 percent from 11.25 percent, even though some economists had recently changed their forecasts for a rise to 12 percent on worsening inflation expectations.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In stark contrast, Mexico's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is seen holding its reference rate unchanged at 4.5 percent on Friday, as policy makers see inflation holding within their target range despite strong economic growth. For a preview, see [ID:nN28268756]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, Mexican \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Governor. Agustin Carstens said in an interview published on Monday that higher agricultural prices could lead policymakers to raise interest rates if they contaminate inflation expectations. [ID:nN28245067]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For Chile's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  minutes in Spanish, see: \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.bcentral.cl/politicas/reuniones-politica-monetaria/pdf/2011/minuta_RPM_febrero2011.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.bcentral.cl/politicas/reuniones-politica-monetaria/pdf/2011/minuta_RPM_febrero2011.pdf}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 (Additional reporting by Juan Lagorio, Antonio de la Jara and Fabian Cambero; Writing by Simon Gardne.Editing by W Simon ) r)\f28 \par \par \uc2 CHILE-CENBANK/ (WRAPUP 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110304e734000x6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
