\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging-Market Assets End Week With 'Lifeless' Session\b0\par\par\uc2 1,139 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Februar 2011\par 21:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --A lackluster day for emerging-market assets\par \par \uc2 --Eastern European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  gave back some recent gains\par \par \uc2 --Emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  broadly firmer against the dollar\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Cynthia Lin \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Despite lingering uncertainties around the Middle East and continuing inflation concerns, emerging-market debt and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were mostly quiet on Friday, heading into a long weekend for the U.S. markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "EM markets seem to have gained some breathing space from the concerns that dominated the past few weeks," said analysts at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "However, we think this relief may be technical and expect rates markets to remain under pressure in the medium term."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Moves on the EM external-debt front were especially muted, as investors continue to gauge various \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate decisions. Although mounting inflationary pressure was the prevalent theme across the developing world since the start of the year, EM debt has more recently been in a holding pattern.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "External debt markets are once again lifeless," RBC analysts noted on Friday, with the latest fund flow data showing investors pulling $741 million out of emerging-market bond funds, according to global fund tracker EPFR. Emerging-market equity funds were also hit with another week of redemptions, with $5.45 million withdrawn by global investors.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The risk premium on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 J.P. Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  Emerging Markets Bond Index Global, or Embig, tightened three basis points to 285 basis points over Treasurys, although it lost 0.14% in price terms on the day. Underlying U.S. Treasury yields were higher on Friday after having fallen to a multiweek low in the prior session amid Middle East concerns.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Argentina's spread narrowed 10 basis points to 528 basis points over Treasurys. Its index fell 0.17% on the day. Analysts at RBC said they shifted the nation's external credit back to overweight based on attractive valuations. Brazil, Mexico, and Peru also each saw slightly lower risk premiums.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At the Group of 20 summit, Brazilian Finance Minister Guido Mantega said Brazil would stand firmly opposed to attempts to limit the scope of emerging economies' use of capital control measures to curb the heavy entrance of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  into their economies and the excessive appreciation of local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The Brazilian real was flat against the dollar on Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Other Latin American \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were generally mixed against the dollar throughout the day, with the Colombian and Chilean peso leading the pack, while others fell slightly.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chile's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Thursday rose rates by 25 basis points, as was largely expected. Gains in Chile's peso, helped by the rate increase, nudged the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  closer to an almost three-year high despite a continuing $12 billion \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  program. On Friday, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Chile\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  purchased $50 million at an average rate of CLP468.71 as part of its dollar-buying program. The peso was recently up 0.26% on the dollar at CLP467.65, according to FactSet.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The usually high-beta Venezuela saw its spread move against the index, up two basis point to 1,127 basis points over Treasurys. Comments from the Venezuelan Oil Minister Rafael Ramirez Thursday confirmed that more dollar debt sales are likely in the pipeline as a measure to control inflation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Despite being recent emerging-market outperformers, Eastern European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  broadly weakened against their benchmark on Friday, coming in as some of the day's biggest laggards.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Turkish lira was weaker against both the U.S. dollar and euro, after having been a recent standout. The euro was up to TRY2.1623 of late from its prior close of TRY2.1434, according to FactSet. The lira was down about 0.2% against the dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Turkey's spread fell six basis points to 204 basis points over the benchmark. The index, however, lost 0.23% on the day. The nation's deputy prime minister said if loan growth exceeds 20% to 25% in February and March, the government may make additional efforts to slow credit expansion. Analysts will be watching next week's credit growth reading.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Early in New York, the Hungarian forint made a sharp turn weaker against the euro, but immediately regained strength. The nation received approval from the U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission to sell $5 billion of debt in the U.S. With a new government in place, Hungary also has plans to raise EUR4 billion this year. The forint is recently hovering around HUF269 against the euro to be flat on the day, based on FactSet.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Czech koruna fell more than 0.4% to the euro, recently at CZK24.397, while the Polish zloty lost about 0.3% to the euro at PLN3.9130 of late.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The South African rand, however, managed to continue its ascent against the dollar, recently up 0.6% at ZAR7.1166 on the dollar. The nation's spread narrowed two basis points to 149 basis points over Treasurys, and gained 0.13% in price terms.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  took over as the leaders, with the group broadly gaining against the dollar. Early in the day, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of China bumped up the nation's bank reserve requirement ratio by 50 basis points as authorities fight to rein in the recent acceleration in headline inflation. That, along with authorities fixing the USD/CNY lower again, set the tone for strong \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  aross the region.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Leading the way were the Korean won, up 0.55% to the dollar, the Indian rupee, up 0.6% to the dollar, and the New Taiwan dollar, up 0.54% to the dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On the Embig, Ghana was one of the few to see its spread expand, up 13 basis points to 393 basis points over benchmark. Its index fell 0.75%. January inflation came in at 9.1% year-over-year, the first increase in nine months. Malaysia was the other notable laggard, with its risk premium increasing eight basis points to 120 basis points over Treasurys. It lost 0.67% on the day. Consumer price data is due out in Malaysia this coming Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gabon also endured a wider spread, up 21 basis points, while losing 1.21% in price terms. The International Monetary Fund is schedule to release its 2010 Article IV Consultation for the unrest-stricken nation on Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Over the weekend, the Group of 20 industrial and developing economies will continue its meeting with the goal of reaching an agreement on setting four indicators as guidelines for measuring global economic imbalances. Also in the week ahead, key emerging-market events include interest rate decisions in Colombia and Hungary, while fourth quarter GDP data is due in Mexico and South Africa.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Cynthia Lin, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-4403; cynthia.lin@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 --Anthony Esposito contributed to this article [ 02-18-11 1517ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020110218e72i0006i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
