\par\par \b \uc2 DJ UPDATE: Without  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2 ,  \highlight22\uc2 Chile \highlight\uc2  Peso Would Be Firmer-Fin Min\b0\par\par\uc2 368 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Februar 2011\par 18:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Updates with additional comments from the Finance Minister)\par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--Without the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Chilean peso would be significantly stronger from where it's trading at now, Finance Minister Felipe Larrain said Thursday.\par \par \uc2 In early January, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced a $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program for 2011, to bring the peso down from the near three-year highs it had been trading at during the fourth quarter of 2010.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been effective, as without it, the peso would be trading 30 to 40 pesos stronger than where it's at," Larrain told reporters.\par \par \uc2 Midday Thursday, the peso was fetching CLP473.10 to the dollar, versus Wednesday's close of CLP473.70 as the market awaited the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary policy decision due out later in the day.\par \par \uc2 Before the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced on Jan. 3 its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, the peso was trading at CLP465.\par \par \uc2 Speaking to reporters, Larrain reiterated that the government won't implement capital controls to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "We aren't considering these measures," he said.\par \par \uc2 In the 1990s, Chile implemented capital controls on foreign investments, which both Larrain and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jose De Gregorio have recently said hadn't been effective.\par \par \uc2 According to Larrain, the government is working on making the peso more competitive by reining in spending with an austere 2011 fiscal budget.\par \par \uc2 "Through the 2011 budget, we gave a clear sign of austerity because we stipulated that spending will grow below gross domestic product growth expectations," he said.\par \par \uc2 He added that in the previous four years, fiscal spending grew at a higher rate than GDP did.\par \par \uc2 For 2011, GDP is expected to grow around 6%. Larrain, who just came back from his southern hemisphere summer vacation, told reporters the year had gotten off to a good start.\par \par \uc2 "We grew nearly 6.5% during the last three quarters of last year and we've started 2011 with growth expectations that top 6%," Larrain said.\par \par \uc2 -By Carolina Pica, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8919; carolina.pica@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 17, 2011 12:51 ET (17:51 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110217e72h005br\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
