\par\par \b \uc2 Chile Peso Ends Firmer On Rising Copper,Rate Hike Expectations\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Esposito   \par \uc2 of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 466 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Februar 2011\par 18:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--Chile's peso ended stronger versus the dollar Monday on the back of rising international copper prices and anticipations for continued interest-rate hikes.\par \par \uc2 The peso ended at CLP469.30 to the dollar, approaching levels not seen since the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced a $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program in early January, compared with CLP472.10 on Friday. It traded in a range of CLP468.80 to CLP473.50.\par \par \uc2 As Chile is the world's largest copper producer and exporter, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market participants usually keep an eye on New York and London copper prices to gauge export revenue inflows and the general health of the Chilean economy.\par \par \uc2 In London trading, copper prices rose 1.7% to $10,131 a metric ton, just shy of last week's record high, on bullish Chinese import data for January.\par \par \uc2 "China's imports left little room for questioning just how strong Chinese demand for copper is. That's boosting the peso and could easily push it to pre-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels in the near-term," said Sergio Tricio, head of studies with \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Chile.\par \par \uc2 Also, increasing inflation expectations have likely set the stage for more interest rate hikes, analysts say. Interest rate hikes tend to foster capital inflows, as participants seek better returns.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president Jose De Gregorio, in a speech in Jerusalem, signaled that controlling inflation is the bank's main goal.\par \par \uc2 "It is important to control inflation, particularly in an environment where prices of foodstuffs and oil have been increasing substantially. The risk of propagation to the rest of inflation has to be mitigated. This is most needed in economies, like Chile, that are operating close to full capacity," De Gregorio said in prepared remarks.\par \par \uc2 He added that the appreciation of emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  "is here to stay. Although their effects on the economy can be mitigated for some time, long-term measures must be adopted to foster competitiveness".\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will hold its next monthly monetary policy meeting on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as part of its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, purchased $50 million at an average rate of CLP470.06. It has accumulated $1.45 billion so far this year.\par \par \uc2 In the bond market, yields on inflation-indexed Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, or BCUs, ended with slight variations in light over-the-counter trading.\par \par \uc2 The yield on five-year BCU bonds slightly higher at 2.70%, from 2.68% on Friday, while the yield on 10-year BCUs closed unchanged at 3.19%.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  (Peso and bond quotes provided by Valor Futuro newswire.) \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Anthony Esposito, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8929; anthony.esposito@dowjones.com [ 14-02-11 1711GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1170 1844   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110214e72e001di\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
