\par\par \b \uc2 MW Mexico unlikely to follow LatAm  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  controls\b0\par\par\uc2 810 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Februar 2011\par 18:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 By Carla Mozee, MarketWatch\par \par \uc2 LOS ANGELES (MarketWatch) -- With a newly lowered inflation forecast, and increasing growth in its economy, Mexico is unlike to follow its Latin American neighbors' moves to hike interest rates and impose capital controls, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso has begun to test levels not seen since the height of the global financial crisis and recently broke through the psychologically key level of 12 pesos per U.S. dollar for the first time since late October 2008.\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Ernesto Cordero has said the country is keeping tabs on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's appreciation but has pointedly said Mexico isn't interested in imposing capital controls.\par \par \uc2 Mexican authorities appear to be content with the peso's rise, according to Marc Chandler, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2  "And why shouldn't they be? A small, gradual increase in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is slow enough so that they can build up their reserves...and by having a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it limits their inflation."\par \par \uc2 With the slow-growth U.S. as its largest trading partner, Mexico stands in contrast to the rapidly expanding Brazilian economy, Latin America's largest.\par \par \uc2 Brazil this year extended its battle against \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation by issuing a reserve requirement for local banks and initiating \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the futures market, as well as continuing daily auctions to purchase dollars on the spot market. In 2009 Brazil raised taxes on certain foreign investments to cool capital inflows.\par \par \uc2 "Mexico has maintained a desire to have a free floating exchange rate for many years," said Clyde Wardle, an emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 HSBC\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mexico's only recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was when it sold dollars during the peak of the credit crisis to protest the peso and the economy from certain stresses. "They've indicated that it doesn't make sense to counter one perceived distortion in the market...perhaps the U.S.'s [second quantitative-easing program]...with another distortion such a capital control," said Wardle.\par \par \uc2 Even with the peso's gains, its advance still lags behind those in other Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Since hitting an all-time low in early March 2009, the peso has risen roughly 20% against the U.S. dollar, trailing the Brazilian real's gain of about 30% during the same period.\par \par \uc2 Chile's peso also outpaced Mexico's over most of the period. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Santiago this year adopted its first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  plan since April 2008, extending through March 8 its plan to make dollar purchases of $50 million a day and the peso has pulled back since the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program was launched last month.\par \par \uc2 While \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said it's not looking to implement capital controls the market "sees a non-negligible chance of the government taking further steps to curb peso appreciation," \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  analyst Maria Belen Olaiz said in a note to clients this week. However, historically high copper prices will likely make it difficult for the Chilean government to avoid further peso strengthening, she said.\par \par \uc2 Alfredo Coutino, \uc2 Moody's\uc2  Economy.com's director of Latin American research said Mexico's current low-inflation environment puts its policy makers at an advantage over regional counterparts who are facing the prospect that interest-rate hikes to combat inflation will further fuel \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation.\par \par \uc2 "Mexican authorities have really not been overly defensive in the media with regard to peso appreciation," said Enrique Alvarez, head of Latin American fixed-income research at IDEAGlobal in New York.\par \par \uc2 He expects the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely act in a "moderate form" and become "increasingly rhetorically defensive," to hedge against the risk of a quick \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise if the peso breaks below the 11.80 level.\par \par \uc2 HSBC\uc2 's call is for the peso to reach 11.80 by the third quarter of this year, said Wardle.\par \par \uc2 Coutino also said the country can take a more relaxed stance compared with its regional competitors partially because Mexico has "lower attractiveness to hot money," stemming in part from its ties to slow growth in the U.S. as well as the low level of speculative returns offered by domestic bonds.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's benchmark interest rate stands at 11.25%, and Argentina's is above 10%. Mexico's benchmark has remained at 4.5% since July of 2009. Coutino said in real terms, the highest return is offered by Brazil, followed by Peru and Chile, and "Mexico at the end."\par \par \uc2 Mexico has tried "almost all kind of capital controls in the past, with very poor results and extremely high costs for the whole economy," Coutino said. "Policy makers know that the short-term benefits of capital controls are not worth enough to compensate for the sacrifice in the future."\par \par \uc2 -Carla Mozee; 415-439-6400; AskNewswires@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 11, 2011 12:05 ET (17:05 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110211e72b004mo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
