\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Latin American  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  gain on Mubarak exit\b0\par\par\uc2 748 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Februar 2011\par 16:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Chile's peso posts 1.4 pct weekly gain\par \par \uc2 * Peru cenbank swats down sol from 33-month high\par \par \uc2 * Speculator bets on Mexican peso rise\par \par \uc2 * Brazil real up 0.24 pct, Mexico peso 0.37 pct (Adds comments, data on Mexican peso CFTC positions)\par \par \uc2 By Michael O'Boyle and Silvio Cascione\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY/SAO PAULO, Feb 11 (Reuters) - Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  firmed on Friday after the resignation of Egyptian President Hosni Mubarak eased fears of unrest in the Middle East that had weighed on demand for riskier assets.\par \par \uc2 Oil and gold prices fell while world stocks rose after Mubarak handed power to the army, curbing concerns that unrest could push up the price of oil and weigh down global growth.\par \par \uc2 "People came to the conclusion it is not that easy to shut down the Suez Canal," said Dirk Willer, head of Latin America local markets strategy at \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  in New York. "If you do not get an oil spike on the back of Egypt developments, since the Suez canal is still open, then it is not a big deal."\par \par \uc2 Chile's peso closed at its strongest since early January, also helped by the view that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will raise interest rates next week.\par \par \uc2 Chile's peso <CLP=> bid 0.28 percent firmer at 472.20 per dollar and notched a 1.4 percent gain for the week. The country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is seen raising its benchmark rate to 3.50 percent next week, resuming hikes after pausing last month in part to avoid further appreciation in the peso.\par \par \uc2 The Chilean peso got a lift earlier this week after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  signaled it has no plans to step up dollar purchases. \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as well as Brazil and Peru, has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets to stem sharp gains in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that are hurting local exporters.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's real <BRBY> bid 0.24 percent firmer at 1.665 per dollar on the local spot market despite two auctions in which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought dollars. The real firmed 0.54 percent for the week.\par \par \uc2 SOL \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 The new government of President Dilma Rousseff introduced spending cuts this week to bring inflation back under control and minimize the need for aggressive interest rate hikes from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . [ID:nN11131412]\par \par \uc2 But with Brazil offering among the highest rates in the world, the cuts have not dented appetite for the real.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso <MXN=> firmed 0.33 percent to 12.0325 per dollar, closing the week with a 0.29 percent loss.\par \par \uc2 Bets by speculators in favor of Mexico's peso edged up to 103,812 contracts in the week ended Feb. 8, according to data from the Commodity Futures Trading Commission in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 The peso has by far the most bets in its favor of the eight major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  tracked by Reuters. It is up around 4 percent since November on the back of an improving outlook for the economy of the United States, Mexico's main trading partner.\par \par \uc2 The large amount of bets on the peso could hurt the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  if U.S. stocks pull back and the hoard of speculators run for the exits, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 "If the S&P were to have a hiccup, then you could see some pressure on the peso," said Willer.\par \par \uc2 Mexico is the one major economy in the region not facing worsening inflation pressures and it is not expected to hike interest rates for some time.\par \par \uc2 Peru's sol <PEN=PE> closed flat after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought $130 million to knock the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  back from a 33-month high.\par \par \uc2 The country raised its benchmark interest rate for the second straight month late on Thursday to 3.5 percent, boosting the appeal of the sol to yield-hungry global investors. Peru's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought $367 million so far in 2011 to undermine gains in the sol.\par \par \uc2 The country is expected to keep hiking interest rates, but it may start to rely on other measures to contain credit growth like raising bank capital ratios.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  concerns should prevent rates from rising (in Peru) to their pre-recession levels and the bulk of further monetary tightening is likely to come through more creative channels," wrote Neil Shearing, an economist at Capital Economics. (Additional reporting by Caroline Stauffer and Ursula Scollo in Lima; Editing by Andrew Hay)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|RNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110214e72b001gn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
