\par\par \b \uc2 Chile Stocks Post Biggest 1-Day Jump Since Mar 2009, End +2.8%\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Esposito   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 523 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Februar 2011\par 21:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--Chile's blue-chip Ipsa index ended 2.8% higher, its biggest single-day surge since March 2009, as Egyptian President Hosni Mubarak's resignation boosted sentiment and investors continued buying following a recent raft of losing sessions.\par \par \uc2 The Ipsa ended higher at 4590.89 points. Meanwhile, volume slipped to 96.0 billion Chilean pesos ($203.4 million), compared to CLP131.3 billion the prior session.\par \par \uc2 Investors went bargain hunting the prior session and remained on the prowl on Friday after the Ipsa had accumulated losses of nearly 11% in the first several weeks of 2011.\par \par \uc2 Also, after 18 days of tense protests in Egypt, global market sentiment improved markedly as the nation's autocratic ruler stepped down, likely averting a violent transition of power.\par \par \uc2 "The events that unfolded in Egypt today helped to quell fears of instability in the [Middle East] region. ... Global instability tends to impact Chile," according to Esteban Navarrete of local brokerage Valenzuela Lafourcade.\par \par \uc2 As Chile imports the majority of fuel it uses, increased fossil fuel prices compound inflation risks and push costs higher. Crude futures had climbed in recent weeks as turmoil in Egypt had sparked worries over whether the Suez Canal could be closed.\par \par \uc2 Among rising shares, fuel and forestry conglomerate Copec (COPEC.SN), the Ipsa's heaviest-weighted share, jumped 4.2% to CLP8,220.00; flagship carrier \uc2 LAN Airlines\uc2  (LFL, LAN.SN) rose 4.4% to CLP13,715.00; and pulp and paper producer CMPC (CMPC.SN) gained 4.8% to CLP23,250.00.\par \par \uc2 Retailers were also among the session's biggest gainers, as expectations for robust demand this year will bode well for them.\par \par \uc2 In the sector, retail holding giant \uc2 Cencosud\uc2  (CENCOSUD.SN) rose 5.7% to CLP3,380.00; the nation's largest department store, Falabella (FALABELLA.SN), increased 1.7% to CLP4,816.00 and retailer La Polar (LAPOLAR.SN) expanded 4.0% to CLP2,900.00.\par \par \uc2 In other local markets, the peso ended stronger versus the dollar, mostly ignoring Mubarak's resignation, as banks and the nation's private pension fund managers sold dollars.\par \par \uc2 The peso ended at CLP472.10 to the dollar, its strongest level since the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced a $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program in early January, compared with CLP473.30 on Thursday. It traded in a range of CLP472.10 to CLP474.70.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as part of its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, purchased $50 million at an average rate of CLP473.43. It has accumulated $1.40 billion so far this year.\par \par \uc2 In the bond market, yields on inflation-indexed Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, or BCUs, ended unchanged on the day in light over-the-counter trading.\par \par \uc2 The yield on five-year BCU bonds ended flat at 2.68%, while the yield on 10-year BCUs closed unchanged at 3.19%.\par \par \uc2 (Peso and bond quotes provided by Valor Futuro newswire.)\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Esposito, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8929; anthony.esposito@dowjones.com [ 11-02-11 2021GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5057   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110211e72b0016t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
