\par\par \b \uc2 Chile Peso Closes Stronger After Piercing Technical Resistance\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Esposito   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 359 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Februar 2011\par 18:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--Chile's peso ended stronger versus the dollar Thursday, ignoring falling copper prices and the euro's slump against the dollar, after breaking a technical level of resistance.\par \par \uc2 The peso ended at CLP473.30 to the dollar, its strongest level since a $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program was announced in early January, compared to CLP476.10 on Wednesday. It traded in a range of CLP473.30 to CLP478.15.\par \par \uc2 After breaking support at CLP477.80, the peso quickly headed for the next level at CLP473.00.\par \par \uc2 "If the peso breaks the next level of resistance, it will likely go to CLP470 and then CLP467 after that, which is practically the same level it was trading at prior to the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Osvaldo Molina, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader with local brokerage and investment bank Larrain Vial.\par \par \uc2 The peso strengthened even though international copper prices fell and the euro lost ground versus the dollar. The peso often takes trading cues from copper prices as Chile is the world's premier producer of the red metal, and from the euro's movements versus the dollar, as nearly one-third of Chilean exports are bound for European ports.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as part of its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, purchased $50 million at an average rate of CLP478.08. It has accumulated $1.35 billion so far this year.\par \par \uc2 In the bond market, yields on inflation-indexed Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, or BCUs, ended mixed after 35 local analysts polled in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's latest monthly economic outlook saw the consumer price index likely gaining 4.0% in 2011 on the year.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sees inflation gaining 3.3% this year, according to its last forecast.\par \par \uc2 The yield on five-year BCU bonds ended at 2.68%, from 2.67% Wednesday, while the yield on 10-year BCUs closed unchanged at 3.19%.\par \par \uc2 (Peso and bond quotes provided by Valor Futuro newswire.)\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Esposito, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8929; anthony.esposito@dowjones.com [ 10-02-11 1700GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1170 1844   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110210e72a001o6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
