\par\par \b \uc2 Chile Stocks Close 1.0% Lower On Continued Profit Taking\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Esposito   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 488 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Februar 2011\par 21:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--Chile's blue-chip Ipsa index continued its slump Tuesday, ending 1% lower as investors took profits and preferred to invest in U.S. and European stocks.\par \par \uc2 The Ipsa ended lower at 4544.24 points, while market volume grew to 83.1 billion Chilean pesos ($173.5 million) from CLP63.6 billion the prior session.\par \par \uc2 Following the Ipsa's record-setting rally last year and considering increasing returns in U.S. and European equity, investors have preferred to book profits in Chile and snap up shares abroad, traders said.\par \par \uc2 The select-stock index has lost a combined 7.8% since December.\par \par \uc2 "Investor confidence is up in the U.S. and Europe, their economic situation is showing plenty of signs of strong recovery that combined with the Ipsa's strong gains last year have prompted participants over the last month to book profits and invest in U.S. and European stocks," said Daniela Game, analyst with local brokerage Corpbanca Corredores de Bolsa.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average, for instance, recently rose to a 30-month high.\par \par \uc2 In Chile, retailers and banks, which were among the biggest gainers in 2010, declined sharply on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Retail holding giant \uc2 Cencosud\uc2  (CENCOSUD.SN) declined 3.4% to CLP3,091.10, the nation's largest department store, Falabella (FALABELLA.SN), fell 0.8% to CLP4,681.80 and retailer Ripley (RIPLEY.SN) lost 1.1% to CLP583.93.\par \par \uc2 Among banks, the largest in terms of loans, \uc2 Banco Santander Chile\uc2  (SAN, BSANTANDE.SN) declined 1.4% to CLP38.55 and the second-largest bank, \uc2 Banco de Chile\uc2  (BCH, CHILE.SN), shed 0.9% to CLP66.70.\par \par \uc2 In other local markets, the peso closed slightly stronger against the dollar, despite tumbling copper prices after China's interest rate increase, as participants wondered whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will increase the daily dollar purchases of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program.\par \par \uc2 The peso ended at CLP478.90 to the dollar compared to CLP479.50 Monday, after trading in a range of CLP478.00 to CLP481.00.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as part of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program for 2011, purchased $50 million at an average rate of CLP479.98 It has accumulated $1.25 billion so far this year.\par \par \uc2 In the bond market, yields on inflation-indexed Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, or BCUs, ended mixed after the consumer price index rose a robust 0.3% on the month in January, in line with market expectations.\par \par \uc2 The yield on five-year BCU bonds ended lower at 2.57% from 2.61% Monday, while the yield on 10-year BCUs closed at 3.20% from 3.17%\par \par \uc2 (Peso and bond quotes provided by Valor Futuro newswire.)\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Esposito, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8929; anthony.esposito@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 --Marcela Castro contributed to this article. [ 08-02-11 2036GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5057   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110208e728001hw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
