\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Brazil yields drop on spending cut bets\b0\par\par\uc2 626 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Februar 2011\par 14:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Brazil real gains 0.77 pct, helped by IPO flows\par \par \uc2 * Mexico peso loses 0.25 pct, \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2  peso flat\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2  cenbank refrains from increasing \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Recasts, adds comments)\par \par \uc2 By Silvio Cascione and Michael O'Boyle\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO/MEXICO CITY, Feb 8 (Reuters) - Interest rates in Brazil fell sharply on Tuesday as investors bet the new government could soon deliver big spending cuts that would curb the need for steep interest rate hikes.\par \par \uc2 Yields on longer-term interest rate futures dropped <0#DIJ:> amid market chatter that the new government could deliver cuts close to 50 billion reais ($83.3 billion) later this week, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 Interest rate futures in Brazil had risen sharply as some investors doubted the government would pony up with enough cuts to help contain rising inflation.\par \par \uc2 "As we get closer to an announcement, the market thinks that they might actually deliver that positive credibility shock that is necessary to reverse a deterioration in expectations," said Flavia Cattan-Naslausky, an analyst at RBS Securities.\par \par \uc2 By clamping down hard on government stimulus, the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not have to raise interest rates as much as the market was expecting in order to contain fears that inflation could spiral out of policymakers' control.\par \par \uc2 The yield on the contract due January 2012 <DIJF2> fell 6 basis points to 12.30 percent while the contract due in January 2013 <DIJF3> shed 17 basis points to 12.68 percent at 4 p.m. local time.\par \par \uc2 IPO FLOWS, CHINA EYED\par \par \uc2 A flurry of initial public offerings this week will probably lure $1 billion into Latin America's largest economy at a time when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to raise interest rates to head off inflation, traders said.\par \par \uc2 "There were some large inflows, from one particular bank that might be IPO-related," said Tony Volpon, head of emerging markets research at Nomura Securities in New York.\par \par \uc2 Many think the government is considering additional steps to arrest the real's 30 percent gain since early 2009. Folha de S.Paulo said on Tuesday that authorities could lift a tax on credit card purchases abroad to discourage imports.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's real <BRBY> added 0.77 percent to 1.665 per dollar in the local spot market.\par \par \uc2 Francisco Carvalho, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at Sao Paulo-based Liquidez Corretora, Brazil's biggest \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  futures broker, "\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be heavier" if the real gains further.\par \par \uc2 Bids for riskier assets were also supported as investors bet an interest rate hike in China would not have a serious impact on China's growth and appetite for raw materials. For details, see [ID:nTOE706030]\par \par \uc2 "Most people do not think that this will derail Chinese growth, if you look at commodity prices they are all pretty much higher. The commodity market is still betting on a soft landing in China," Volpon added.\par \par \uc2 China is Brazil's top trading partner and a major consumer of the region's commodities such as iron, copper and oil.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  <MXN=> shed 0.25 percent to 12.05 per dollar, reversing earlier gains that had the peso trading near a 2-1/2-year low hit last month.\par \par \uc2 Chile's peso <CLP=>, which has also been gaining despite government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , closed flat at 479 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Investors moved to the sidelines ahead of a decision on from the Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the size of its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program.\par \par \uc2 Some said policymakers could raise the amount of daily dollar purchases, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Tuesday after the market close that it would maintain its $50 million daily pace. [ID:nSAG002719]\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|U|D|M|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110211e728000wx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
