\par\par \b \uc2 DAVOS-Chile finmin says markets correct to see rate rises\b0\par\par\uc2 356 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Januar 2011\par 08:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 DAVOS, Switzerland, Jan 27 (Reuters) - Chilean Finance Minister Felipe Larrain said on Thursday that markets were right to expect more rate rises from Chile but that the country was not considering capital controls to tame peso appreciation.\par \par \uc2 "We won't have any surprises in terms of increased \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 " on top of the $12 billion of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  already announced, Larrain told Reuters at the Davos World Economic Forum annual meeting.\par \par \uc2 Asked about further measures to rein in the Chilean peso, Larrain said: "We are not considering capital controls."\par \par \uc2 He emphasised instead keeping a tight rein on spending, using a diversified package of financing and finding different kinds of derivative for small business to cover exchange risk.\par \par \uc2 Larrain said Chile would stick to its 2-4 percent inflation target. Asked about interest rate rises, he replied:\par \par \uc2 "You can probably expect more rate increases just because we have now a rate which is 3.25 percent and with three per cent inflation, that is still a very expansionary monetary policy."\par \par \uc2 Referring to expectations of a gradual rise in interest rates during the year, Larrain said: "That is internalised by the markets, that's what they are expecting and they are expecting correctly.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  began a record $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year to stop export-damaging strength in the peso.\par \par \uc2 Much of Latin America is grappling with rising food prices, which have been largely countered in Chile by the strong peso \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Seven successive rate hikes have lured foreign investment into local financial markets, one factor that helped propel the peso up 17 percent in the second half of 2010 to its strongest in nearly three years.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left benchmark interest rates steady at 3.25 percent on Jan 13 after raising it for seven consecutive months, in a move widely seen complementing its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in curbing the peso's strength.\par \par \uc2 Consumer prices in \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose 3 percent last year, in line with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's inflation target. (Reporting and writing by Michael Stott)\par \par \uc2 DAVOS/CHILE|LANGEN|ABN|M|D   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110130e71r00029\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
