\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:  \highlight22\uc2 Chile \highlight\uc2  Struggling To Balance  \highlight22\uc2 Forex Intervention \highlight\uc2  - RBC\b0\par\par\uc2 1,245 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Januar 2011\par 18:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in New York.  Robert Flint, 212-416-2216; \par robert.flint@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1739 GMT [Dow Jones] Chilean authorities are struggling to juggle the conflicting objectives of further integrating Chile "with global financial markets and curtailing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciation," \f28 \uc2 \f2 RBC Capital Markets\f28 \uc2 \f2  said in research note. The bank is currently boosting foreign reserves by $12 billion to bring the peso down from its recent 32-month highs. If the peso continues to strengthen, the bank could potentially expand its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserve accumulation during 2011 by $5 billion-to-$10 billion. Chile will likely continue to implement a combo of measures to support exporters, which see their competitiveness hurt by a strong peso, "such as speeding up the export process, increasing their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves in a transparent manner through scheduled USD purchases and possibly looking to further balance capital inflows with outflows, avoiding repatriation," RBC said. (anthony.esposito@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1732 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P weighs into the ongoing discussion over whether states should be allowed to file for bankruptcy. In a note today, the ratings agency said it's unlikely a state would consider a bankruptcy filing, given the negative financial implications. But, if states are allowed to file for bankruptcy, S&P would evaluate its criteria since not being a able to file is "fundamental to our analysis of the sector." S&P adds, "it should be noted that the ability to file and the practicality of such filings would be a key component of our assessment of the credit quality of the sector."\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (romy.varghese@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1731 GMT [Dow Jones] State and local governments may have budget problems, but they don't have a debt problem, says DWS Investments' Phil Condon in press conference call. Debt service typically makes up just 5% to 8% of state and local governments' budgets, meaning they "are really not that overleveraged," Condon says. Indeed, if state and local governments had no debt, they would "still have a problem," he says. And while a difficult budget season is ahead, "what I see more and more is governments facing up to their responsibilities," Condon says, noting Illinois' recent increase in its income tax and California's Jerry Brown taking a hard line on the Golden State's financial issues.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (kelly.nolan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1728 GMT [Dow Jones] The Treasury market is setting up for the 5-year sale with bond prices getting another leg down. The rising yields will be what the auction needs to lure buyers given the sale will come shortly before the FOMC statement. In recent trade, the benchmark 10-yr note is 20/32 lower to yield 3.411%. (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1728 GMT [Dow Jones] Treasury and longer-dated Eurodollar futures sink to intraday lows as contracts become cheaper before $35 bln 5-yr Tsy note auction. FOMC announcement also looms. Mar 10-yr Tsy notes drop to 120-06+, lifting 10-yr cash yield equivalent above 3.375%. Support seen at Tues' low of 120-03+. Quarterly 2012 contracts recently down 4 to 7.5 BP. 2013 through 2014 contracts recently 8 to 10 BP lower. (howard.packowitz@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1727 GMT [Dow Jones] US interest rates will go up and the country's economic recovery will be choked off when the Federal Reserve ends its quantitative easing program, investor George Soros said in an interview on CNBC from Davos, Switzerland. Additionally, Soros said the US municipal debt problem would provide the economic "drama" story for the year. Municipal services, he said, would be cut and pressure will build on unions to renegotiate contracts.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1725 GMT [Dow Jones] CAD is little changed vs USD, holding earlier gains and trading within a narrow band, as investors await news from the Federal Reserve. USD is at 0.9956, from 0.9977 late Tues, according to CQG. The market is focused on the Fed's 1915 GMT (2:15 p.m. EST) policy decision. The market is expecting no change in policy and little change, if any, in the accompanying statement. Investors will parse the Fed's language for hints of a willingness to alter or shorten its controversial $600-billion monetary-stimulus program. (karen.johnson@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1706 GMT [Dow Jones] Stock market rally failed to alter sentiment in fed-funds futures that FOMC probably will not raise funds rate this year. Jan 2012 fed-funds contract, measuring end-of-2011 rate outlook, prices in only 40% chance for 0.5% rate. That's down from 46% chance at Fri's setlement. Same contract priced in 98% chance as recently as Jan. 5 settlement. That was before the weaker-than-expected Dec. jobs report and indications from Fed members that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will complete its QE2 Tsy purchases. (howard.packowitz@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1704 GMT [Dow Jones] State and local governments aren't facing debt problems, such as onerous interest payments, \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  economists say in a report. "Instead, governors, mayors and county executives are grappling with budget problems that must be resolved through a combination of new revenues, spending cuts, or federal assistance," they say. The economists consider the risk of widespread municipal defaults low, although headline risk will persist this year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (romy.varghese@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1702 GMT [Dow Jones] The euro edged marginally higher as European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  president Jean-Claude Trichet said Wednesday during a Bloomberg TV interview that he would not exclude EFSF from buying sovereign debt as a tool to help Europe through the ongoing debt crisis. The euro is at $1.3689 in late morning trading in New York, from $1.3682 late Tuesday, according to EBS via CQG.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (stephen.bernard@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1646 GMT [Dow Jones] Another Treasury sale that will come shortly before the FOMC statement and that is likely to keep some bidders on the sidelines, traders say. Good new home sales data may also dent demand for safe assets. A support comes from higher yields with the WI five-year is now trading at 2.047%, higher than the 2.006% on the current benchmark 5-yr note, showing some concessions. A key test comes on buy-side demand, especially the indirect bids -- a proxy of foreign demand. Foreign buying has been declining, taking 22.6% at the last four auctions vs. 24.6% at the prior four. the bid-to-cover ratio averages 2.76 for the recent four 5-yr sales. (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1643 GMT [Dow Jones] Czech inflation is on the rise but it's still too early to say whether it is a serious issue, the Czech \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governor said, adding that the outlook for the country's export-driven economy is rosy. "At this moment we can see signs of inflationary pressures," Miroslav Singersaid during a university lecture just a week ahead of the bank's interest rate setting meeting, but noted that it is, however, difficult to predict how strong the pressures will become. The 2010 trade surplus, the second largest ever, shows the country's economy is roaring ahead, Singer said. The bank has kept inflation under control for sometime, but recent data have pointed to a solid economic recovery which in turn is stoking inflationary pressures. In December, annual inflation accelerated to 2.3% from 2% in November.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (sean.carney@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 01-26-11 1239ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110126e71q000b9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
