\par\par \b \uc2 *DJ  \highlight22\uc2 Chile Central Bank \highlight\uc2  President De Gregorio: Peso Responding To  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  As Expected\b0\par\par\uc2 821 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Januar 2011\par 20:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 24 Jan 2011 14:36 EDT *DJ  \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President: Capital Controls Not Currently Needed\par \par \uc2 24 Jan 2011 14:36 EDT *DJ  \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President: \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Was Based On Long-Term Outlook\par \par \uc2 24 Jan 2011 14:36 EDT *DJ  \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President: Global Reserve Increase Helped \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Timing\par \par \uc2 24 Jan 2011 14:36 EDT *DJ  \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President: Economy Already Seeing \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Costs\par \par \uc2 24 Jan 2011 14:47 EDT =DJ  \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Pres De Gregorio: Peso Responding To \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  As Expected\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     By Carolina Pica \par     Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--The Chilean peso has responded as expected to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the bank's president Jose De Gregorio said Monday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The peso, which for most of the last quarter traded at 32-month highs against the dollar, has depreciated about 5% since the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  announced its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  program in early January.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Noting that a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could never rule out anything, De Gregorio told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview that capital controls weren't currently needed.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It's not evident" that Chile would require capital controls in the immediate future as the country is currently a net creditor, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He added that Chile's experience with capital controls in the 1990s shows they weren't very effective as they didn't curb inflows, but just changed the type of inflows.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "They didn't buffer us from the effects of the Asian crisis [in the late 1990s]," he said in reference to the recession into which Chile fell at the end of that decade.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The central banker says capital controls can be effective in certain circumstances, but that for the most part, they produce a dichotomy of sorts.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Countries say they want long-term capital but not speculative capital. How do you distinguish between the two?" he asked. "They say 'we want capital, but not appreciation,' which in my view is a dichotomous view that can easily become an inconsistency."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As to the bank's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , through which it plans to buy a total $12 billion this year to increase reserves to 17% of gross domestic product, De Gregorio said the decision to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was based on a long-term outlook, not a specific trading level for the peso.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It would be a mistake to think that this \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was contingent on a specific nominal value for the peso," De Gregorio said. "It had to do with the peso's outlook and we couldn't rule out that the peso would remain strong for a prolonged time, and that this could have implications," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Many of Chile's economic sectors, such as agriculture and industry, were losing competitiveness abroad on the peso's strength.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He added that the global context showed many other countries are increasing their reserves, so it made sense for Chile to make the same move.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "To a certain degree, what's happening around the world in terms of reserves pushes us to increase ours in order to improve our safeguards, so the timing for our move was right," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But he reiterated that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market has its costs, as people are already seeing some import prices, such as gasoline at the pump, increase. Chile imports the vast majority of fuels that it consumes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While De Gregorio admitted that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could push inflation marginally higher, he doesn't see this modifying the bank's monetary policy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The [inflationary] effect is buffered and compensated for," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After applying an unprecedented stimulus which brought the benchmark overnight rate to a record low 0.5% in mid-2009 and included quantitative easing measures, the bank began removing this stimulus in June of 2010.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The benchmark rate is currently at 3.25%. Analysts expect the rate to end the year at 5%, according to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's monthly poll of analysts' expectations.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Regarding the measures his colleagues at the U.S. Federal Reserve have taken, De Gregorio sees the U.S. economy recovering as a result of the strong stimulus.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He downplays the effects that the Fed's quantitative easing has had on emerging markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "In the international economic and political scene, the effects of U.S. QE and China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy tend to be overstated. While I don't want to undermine their global impact, they aren't at the heart of matter," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The central banker believes the Chinese yuan, whose strength he says comes from sizable domestic savings, will eventually appreciate, albeit gradually.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "That should come on the back of increased domestic spending in that country and that should ease some of the pressure," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Carolina Pica, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8919; carolina.pica@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 January 24, 2011 14:47 ET (19:47 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110124e71o0053h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
